{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00987 - MONDO_20320",
  "verbalization": "Cytarabine is associated with Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia With Maturation through a series of indication pathways that link the drug to other agents, which in turn are linked to this leukemia subtype. The evidence suggests that Cytarabine is used to treat Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia Without Maturation, a closely related condition, and that this condition is linked to Gilteritinib, Enasidenib, and Quizartinib, all of which are indicated for Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia With Maturation. This implies a potential pathway where Cytarabine may be repurposed or co-administered with these agents to treat the more mature form of the disease. The high scores in the paths suggest a strong, though indirect, relationship, likely mediated by the shared indication for Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia Without Maturation. The mechanism may involve the drug’s ability to induce cell death in leukemia cells, which could be relevant across different subtypes, though the exact molecular mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
